1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,609
A bicycle wheel keeps most
of its weight at the rim.

2
00:00:03,609 --> 00:00:06,066
A machine flywheel is heavy at its edge.

3
00:00:06,066 --> 00:00:08,847
A grinding stone is solid
all the way through.

4
00:00:09,247 --> 00:00:13,518
Same idea every time: where the
mass sits changes how it spins.

5
00:00:13,968 --> 00:00:15,908
Aarav has a ring and a disc.

6
00:00:15,908 --> 00:00:16,808
Same mass.

7
00:00:16,723 --> 00:00:17,623
Same size.

8
00:00:17,538 --> 00:00:19,421
He gives each the same push.

9
00:00:19,771 --> 00:00:21,497
The disc whirls faster.

10
00:00:21,497 --> 00:00:23,012
The ring is lazier.

11
00:00:23,012 --> 00:00:24,581
Same mass, so why?

12
00:00:25,081 --> 00:00:29,330
To compare them in one number,
we use the radius of gyration.

13
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:33,384
Squeeze all of a body's mass
into a thin ring around the axis.

14
00:00:33,734 --> 00:00:37,428
Choose the ring's radius so that its
moment of inertia matches the body's.

15
00:00:37,828 --> 00:00:40,901
That radius is the radius of gyration, k.

16
00:00:40,901 --> 00:00:42,831
So I equals M k squared.

17
00:00:43,231 --> 00:00:47,314
k tells you how far out the mass sits,
on average, in the way that counts

18
00:00:47,314 --> 00:00:48,214
for turning.

19
00:00:48,729 --> 00:00:50,157
Our motion notebook.

20
00:00:50,157 --> 00:00:53,712
A disc of mass two kilograms
and radius half a metre.

21
00:00:53,712 --> 00:00:55,679
Its I is half MR squared.

22
00:00:55,679 --> 00:00:56,857
Point two five.

23
00:00:57,207 --> 00:01:01,727
Now take a ring with the same two
kilograms, at the full half metre.

24
00:01:01,727 --> 00:01:03,362
Its I is MR squared.

25
00:01:03,362 --> 00:01:04,268
Point five.

26
00:01:04,268 --> 00:01:05,168
Too big.

27
00:01:05,426 --> 00:01:06,641
Shrink the ring.

28
00:01:06,641 --> 00:01:09,388
Its I falls fast, because r is squared.

29
00:01:09,388 --> 00:01:11,779
Watch the dot slide down the curve.

30
00:01:12,179 --> 00:01:14,152
Slow-mo replay, near the match.

31
00:01:14,502 --> 00:01:16,683
Here the ring's I equals the disc's I.

32
00:01:16,683 --> 00:01:17,701
Point two five.

33
00:01:17,701 --> 00:01:18,761
The lines cross.

34
00:01:19,161 --> 00:01:21,923
The radius is point
three five four metres.

35
00:01:21,923 --> 00:01:24,340
That is the disc's radius of gyration.

36
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:29,400
It is less than the full radius,
because a disc has much of its mass near

37
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:30,300
the centre.

38
00:01:30,861 --> 00:01:33,706
Let's find k for a disc, step by step.

39
00:01:34,056 --> 00:01:34,956
Step one.

40
00:01:34,876 --> 00:01:37,556
By its meaning, I equals M k squared.

41
00:01:38,306 --> 00:01:39,206
Step two.

42
00:01:39,063 --> 00:01:41,694
So k equals the square root of I over M.

43
00:01:42,444 --> 00:01:43,358
Step three.

44
00:01:43,358 --> 00:01:46,955
For a disc about its centre,
I is half MR squared.

45
00:01:46,955 --> 00:01:50,294
Put it in: M k squared
equals half MR squared.

46
00:01:51,044 --> 00:01:51,984
Step four.

47
00:01:51,984 --> 00:01:52,884
Cancel M.

48
00:01:52,870 --> 00:01:55,322
k squared is R squared over two.

49
00:01:55,322 --> 00:01:58,845
So k is R over root two,
about point seven R.

50
00:01:59,595 --> 00:02:02,454
Half a metre times point seven zero seven.

51
00:02:02,454 --> 00:02:04,061
Point three five four.

52
00:02:04,061 --> 00:02:05,627
The same as the sim.

53
00:02:06,227 --> 00:02:07,246
Remember this.

54
00:02:07,246 --> 00:02:11,579
The radius of gyration is where all
the mass could sit and give the same

55
00:02:11,579 --> 00:02:12,846
moment of inertia.

56
00:02:13,546 --> 00:02:16,558
Now, why the disc's k is
smaller, link by link.

57
00:02:16,908 --> 00:02:20,199
In a ring, every bit of mass
sits at the full radius.

58
00:02:20,599 --> 00:02:23,442
In a disc, many bits
sit close to the centre.

59
00:02:23,842 --> 00:02:27,914
Bits near the axis add very
little, because r is squared.

60
00:02:28,314 --> 00:02:32,447
So the disc has a smaller I,
and its k sits inside the rim.

61
00:02:33,047 --> 00:02:36,245
Here are the common bodies you
must know, from the NCERT table.

62
00:02:36,595 --> 00:02:39,925
A ring about its central axis: MR squared.

63
00:02:39,925 --> 00:02:42,072
A disc: half MR squared.

64
00:02:42,472 --> 00:02:46,274
A thin rod about its middle:
ML squared over twelve.

65
00:02:46,674 --> 00:02:50,493
A solid sphere about a
diameter: two fifths MR squared.

66
00:02:51,093 --> 00:02:52,164
Side by side.

67
00:02:52,164 --> 00:02:55,218
Ring and disc, two
kilograms, half a metre.

68
00:02:55,218 --> 00:02:57,310
Give each one joule of energy.

69
00:02:57,710 --> 00:03:01,587
The ring, with I point five,
turns at two radians per second.

70
00:03:01,587 --> 00:03:05,326
The disc, with half the I,
turns at two point eight three.

71
00:03:05,926 --> 00:03:06,944
Now you try.

72
00:03:06,944 --> 00:03:10,454
A solid sphere and a disc,
same mass, same radius.

73
00:03:10,454 --> 00:03:14,369
Which has the smaller moment of
inertia about a central axis?

74
00:03:14,369 --> 00:03:15,635
Pause and decide.

75
00:03:18,835 --> 00:03:19,735
The sphere!

76
00:03:19,576 --> 00:03:21,446
Two fifths is less than one half.

77
00:03:21,446 --> 00:03:23,984
More of a sphere's mass
sits close to the axis.

78
00:03:24,634 --> 00:03:25,534
One more.

79
00:03:25,435 --> 00:03:30,108
A hollow cylinder and a ring, same
mass, same radius, about their central

80
00:03:30,108 --> 00:03:31,008
axis.

81
00:03:30,656 --> 00:03:32,318
Which has the bigger I?

82
00:03:32,318 --> 00:03:33,569
Pause and decide.

83
00:03:36,769 --> 00:03:37,669
Neither!

84
00:03:37,443 --> 00:03:38,960
Both are MR squared.

85
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:42,241
In both, every bit of mass
is at the full radius.

86
00:03:42,241 --> 00:03:44,678
Length along the axis does not matter.

87
00:03:45,328 --> 00:03:47,018
Now, one worked example.

88
00:03:47,368 --> 00:03:52,487
A uniform rod is one point two metres
long, with a mass of two kilograms.

89
00:03:52,487 --> 00:03:56,999
Find its moment of inertia and
radius of gyration about its middle.

90
00:03:57,499 --> 00:03:59,915
I equals ML squared over twelve.

91
00:03:59,915 --> 00:04:03,017
Two times one point
four four, over twelve.

92
00:04:03,017 --> 00:04:04,246
Point two four.

93
00:04:04,996 --> 00:04:06,995
k equals root of I over M.

94
00:04:06,995 --> 00:04:09,299
Root of point two four over two.

95
00:04:09,299 --> 00:04:10,974
Root of point one two.

96
00:04:11,724 --> 00:04:14,729
So k is about point three four six metres.

97
00:04:15,379 --> 00:04:16,840
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:17,190 --> 00:04:20,315
Many students write k equals
R over two for a disc.

99
00:04:20,715 --> 00:04:24,167
About its central axis,
k is R over root two.

100
00:04:24,167 --> 00:04:27,738
R over two is only for
an axis along a diameter.

101
00:04:27,738 --> 00:04:29,454
Always check the axis.

102
00:04:30,104 --> 00:04:31,424
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:31,424 --> 00:04:35,296
In ratio questions between common
bodies, and in finding k from I.

104
00:04:35,896 --> 00:04:37,047
Exam level now.

105
00:04:37,047 --> 00:04:41,621
A solid sphere about a diameter, and
a ring about its central axis, have

106
00:04:41,621 --> 00:04:43,522
the same radius of gyration.

107
00:04:43,522 --> 00:04:47,189
Find the ratio of the sphere's
radius to the ring's radius.

108
00:04:47,189 --> 00:04:48,389
Pause and solve.

109
00:04:53,789 --> 00:04:55,166
The answer is B.

110
00:04:55,166 --> 00:04:56,927
Root of five over two.

111
00:04:57,427 --> 00:04:59,967
For the ring, k equals its radius.

112
00:05:00,467 --> 00:05:04,160
For the sphere, k squared is two
fifths of its radius squared.

113
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:05,662
Set them equal.

114
00:05:05,662 --> 00:05:08,989
The sphere's radius over the
ring's is root of five over two.

115
00:05:08,989 --> 00:05:10,602
About one point five eight.

116
00:05:11,202 --> 00:05:12,743
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:12,743 --> 00:05:14,326
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:17,526 --> 00:05:19,465
Can you answer these without help?

119
00:05:19,865 --> 00:05:20,765
One.

120
00:05:20,304 --> 00:05:23,102
What does the radius of
gyration of a body mean?

121
00:05:24,002 --> 00:05:24,902
Two.

122
00:05:24,444 --> 00:05:27,616
Why is a disc's radius of
gyration less than its radius?

123
00:05:28,516 --> 00:05:29,416
Three.

124
00:05:29,099 --> 00:05:33,504
Why do a ring and a hollow cylinder
of the same mass and radius have the

125
00:05:33,504 --> 00:05:34,404
same I?

126
00:05:35,092 --> 00:05:39,943
Next time: spin a coin flat, then on
its edge, then about a point on its

127
00:05:39,943 --> 00:05:40,843
rim.

128
00:05:40,447 --> 00:05:42,927
Three different answers from one coin.

129
00:05:42,927 --> 00:05:45,961
The theorems of perpendicular
and parallel axes.
